+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-application-semantics
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch application semantics"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+# JSON Patch application semantics
+
+## Context
+`applyPatch` must follow RFC 6902, reuse the RFC 6901 pointer parsing of dl-001, and not mutate input.
+
+## Decision
+- The document is deep-cloned (`structuredClone`) once, operations mutate the clone in order, and
+  `value`s taken from the patch or copied are cloned too. Any failure throws, so no partial result escapes.
+- The whole patch is validated first (array of objects, known `op`, string `path`/`from` that are valid
+  pointers, `value` present where required); unknown extra members are ignored (RFC 6902 A.11).
+- `-` is accepted only as the last token of an `add` target (append); array indexes must be canonical and
+  `<= length` for add, `< length` otherwise. dl-001's `resolvePointer` is unchanged; patch code uses the
+  shared `parseTokens` internally.
+- `remove`/`replace`/`move`/`copy` need an existing target/source; `remove` of the root fails, `replace`
+  or `add` at `""` replaces the document. `move` onto itself is a no-op; into its own child is an error.
+- `test` uses deep JSON equality, ignoring object key order, with strict types (`1` is not `"1"`).
+- Members are set with `defineProperty` and looked up as own properties, so `__proto__` is plain data.
+
+## Consequences
+Callers get atomic, side-effect-free patching. Operation values must be structured-cloneable JSON.
